@eliebakouch @privitera_ @raphaeldoan @philip_fox_ Being a few months behind is a very reasonable objective that helps with many of the problems (though not all), and we discuss + suggest this elsewhere! this is specifically for actual frontier parity - i.e. stuff that's as good as whatever OAI + Ants internal models are - and for that, I think the numbers are the right ballpark, both in terms of staff and compute. 

One core assumption being that you no longer get access to rented hyperscaler compute past some point of frontier-building because of US intervention. The numbers are very high in part because of that prospect: this needs to be serious enough that it's very likely to work; you might be able to skirt by with less, but it's not assured, and if it isn't above a threshold of success odds, you just end up having antagonised the US and still not having a frontier model. 

I think actual frontier parity is almost prohibitively costly, and it strikes me as extremely valuable to clarify this to European policymakers: if they think access to the actual frontier is important, they either need to get it from the US or be ready to spend in this ballpark. Any uncertainty about this, I think, incentivises halfhearted frontier projects that leave us isolated.

